# M10 -- ostrya's CLI surface against the ostree tool's.
#
# A cell is one CLI invocation, so each record names a subcommand, a `cell`
# identifier tail, and the command line itself, in place of M0/M1's
# `modes`/`corpus` product. See README.md, "M10 record format", for the field
# reference and the outcome/oracle vocabulary, and harness.md for the setup
# vocabulary, the placeholders, and the assertion forms.
#
# The harness gives each implementation its own scratch subtree and runs the
# `run:` line in it, so `$REPO` is that side's own repository path. Two cells
# control the working directory and the environment of the invocation, which a
# `run:` line does not state; each names a probe.
#
# This file carries Phase 17a's cases -- `init` and the global `--repo`
# conventions -- Phase 17b's -- `refs`, `rev-parse`, and `cat` -- and Phase
# 17b1's -- `commit` parenting (`../port-plan.md`). Later sub-phases add records
# here as their own subcommands and options land.
#
# The `repo-with-commit` setup binds one branch, `conformance`, holding one
# commit of corpus `C0`, so a cell needing a second ref, a nested ref name, an
# alias, or a collection id cannot state itself with a `run:` line against it. A
# cell reading a commit's own parent cannot either: the parent is a field of the
# commit object, so a second invocation reads it. Those cells cite `evidence:`
# instead, naming the `crates/ostrya-cli/tests/cli.rs` test that compares the
# port against the tool over a repository built for the case. Growing the
# setup vocabulary would let them become `run:` cells; that is a harness
# change, not a Phase 17b one.
#
# No Phase 17b cell names the `checksum-agreement` oracle. Each side's setup
# commits with its own binary and neither passes a timestamp, so the two commit
# checksums differ by wall-clock time until `commit --timestamp` lands in Phase
# 17c. A cell that states a checksum instead names `stdout-text`, whose
# normalizer turns the bound `$REV` into the placeholder, so
# `rev-parse conformance` printing `$REV` on both sides states that each
# implementation resolved the branch to the commit its own setup made.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# init -- mode acceptance.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=bare
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=bare
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#p0-blocks-the-harness

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=bare-user
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=bare-user
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#p0-blocks-the-harness

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=bare-user-only
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=bare-user-only
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#p0-blocks-the-harness

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=archive
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=archive
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#p0-blocks-the-harness
note: `--mode=archive` and `--mode=archive-z2` name the same mode, which both
 implementations serialize as `archive-z2`. The `config-bytes` oracle states
 that the port's config equals the tool's for this spelling, and the
 `init/mode=archive-z2` cell below states the same for the other one, so the
 two cells together state that the port normalizes each spelling the way the
 tool does. Neither cell reads the `mode` value itself; that the value is
 `archive-z2` is `format-reference.md`'s claim.

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=archive-z2
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=archive-z2
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#p0-blocks-the-harness
note: the alias both implementations accept alongside `archive`. The cell
 pairs with `init/mode=archive` above: each states that the port's config
 equals the tool's for one spelling.

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=bare-user-shared
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=bare-user-shared
ref-run: n-a
expect-exit: 0
expect-stderr: empty
oracle: exit-status config-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#port-extension-bare-user-shared-mode
note: `bare-user-shared` is a port extension the tool has no `--mode` value
 for; the tool refuses to even open a repository ostrya created in this mode
 (`error: opening repo: Invalid mode 'bare-user-shared' in repository
 configuration`), already covered by the D2 direction of
 `m1-operate.matrix`. `ref-run: n-a` leaves the port's own claims as the
 cell's only assertions: the mode is accepted and the repository is created.
 The oracles record the port's artifacts and compare nothing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# init -- rejection, and the cwd/OSTREE_REPO fallback it shares with every
# other subcommand.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/mode=bogus
setup: empty-dir
run: init --repo=$REPO --mode=bogus
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid mode 'bogus' in repository configuration"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid mode 'bogus' in repository configuration"
oracle: exit-status stderr-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: both implementations write that one line to standard error and leave no
 repository behind, so the `stderr-text` oracle compares the whole stream.

family: M10
subcommand: init
cell: init/fallback-reuse
setup: empty-dir
probe: init-reuse-via-cwd-and-env
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: the probe runs `init` with the repository as the current directory, and
 again through `OSTREE_REPO` with a non-repository current directory. Both
 implementations reuse the existing repository idempotently (exit 0, config
 unchanged) rather than requiring an explicit `--repo`: `init` shares the
 cwd/`OSTREE_REPO` precedence every other subcommand uses. The probe builds
 the repository with `collection-id` set, so the cell does not also exercise
 the tool's fallback-only crash on a repository lacking one (`error: Key file
 does not have key "collection-id" in group "core"`), which the identical
 reuse through an explicit `--repo` does not produce and which the port does
 not reproduce (cli-surface.md, "Global conventions"). The cell carries no
 claim about that divergence either way. A probe states this cell because the
 working directory and the environment are what it varies, and a `run:` line
 states neither.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global --repo/OSTREE_REPO conventions, exercised through subcommands Phase
# 12 and earlier already implement, since Phase 17a adds no reading
# subcommand of its own.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: prune
cell: prune/repo-position-precedence
setup: repo
probe: repo-position-precedence
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: the same repository resolves whether `--repo` is given before the
 subcommand, after it, or via `OSTREE_REPO` with no `--repo` at all and a
 non-repository current directory; all three forms report the same object
 count, for both implementations. A probe states this cell for the same
 reason as `init/fallback-reuse`.

family: M10
subcommand: export
cell: export/trailing-repo-wins
setup: two-repos
run: --repo=$REPO export --repo=$REPO2 $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: contains "distinguish-repo-2"
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: contains "distinguish-repo-2"
oracle: exit-status
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: the `two-repos` setup commits a distinguishing marker into each
 repository under one branch name. Both implementations export the
 subcommand-position repository, so the tar stream on standard output holds
 the second marker and not the first. The two streams are not compared: the
 claim is which repository was read, and the tar bytes carry more than that.

family: M10
subcommand: static-delta
cell: static-delta/no-subcommand
setup: empty-dir
run: static-delta --repo=$REPO
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: No command specified"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: No command specified"
oracle: exit-status
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: `static-delta` takes a nested subcommand, and leaving it unnamed is
 reported before the repository is resolved. `empty-dir` binds `$REPO` to a
 path that does not exist, so a repository check running first would report
 `error: opening repo` instead; both implementations report the missing
 subcommand. Each prints the subcommand's usage text before the error line,
 which `stderr-text` is not an oracle for here, for the same reason as
 `prune/no-repo-resolvable` below.

family: M10
subcommand: prune
cell: prune/no-repo-resolvable
run: prune
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: Command requires a --repo argument"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Command requires a --repo argument"
oracle: exit-status
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: cli-surface.md#global-conventions
note: the cell runs in its own scratch root, which is not a repository, with
 no `--repo` and no `OSTREE_REPO`. Both implementations print the
 subcommand's usage text to standard error, then that error line, and exit 1.
 The port's usage text is clap's own rendering and the tool's is GOption's, so
 the shared claim is the shape -- usage, then the error line, then exit 1 --
 which is why `stderr-text` is not an oracle here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# refs -- listing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/list
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: equals "$BRANCH"
ref-expect-stdout: equals "$BRANCH"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the default listing prints one refspec per line, with no prefix given and
 nothing stripped. The setup binds one branch, so the whole listing is that one
 name, which both implementations print alone.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/list-revision
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -r
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: contains "$BRANCH"
ref-expect-stdout: contains "$BRANCH"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: `-r` appends a tab and the commit checksum. The `stdout-text` oracle
 carries the exact comparison, the normalizer having turned each side's own
 commit checksum into `$REV`, so the cell states both the separator and the
 value each side resolved. The `contains` claims state the name alone, a tab
 being awkward to write in a record. Each claim reads `$BRANCH`, which is what
 the ref name normalizes to, the branch name being a bound placeholder value.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-exact-match
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: equals "$BRANCH"
ref-expect-stdout: equals "$BRANCH"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a PREFIX equal to the ref prints the whole refspec and strips nothing,
 which is the boundary case of the stripping rule. A prefix the ref is nested
 under, and `--list` suppressing the stripping, need a nested ref name the
 setup does not bind; `refs/nested-prefix-stripping` below cites those.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-no-match
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO nosuch
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: empty
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a prefix matching nothing is not an error in either implementation.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-invalid
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO test/
expect-exit: 1
expect-stdout: empty
expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid refspec test/"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Listing refs: Invalid refspec test/"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a PREFIX no ref name can hold is refused before it matches anything, so a
 trailing `/` ends the listing rather than selecting the empty set. Both refuse
 it and exit 1, in different words: the tool prefixes the line with `Listing
 refs: ` and the port reports the refspec rule's one message, so `stderr-text`
 is not an oracle here (`cli-surface.md`, "P1"). The remaining refused forms and
 the ordering the check keeps are `refs/prefix-invalid-forms` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-invalid-forms
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_refuses_an_invalid_prefix
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the prefix-validation cases a setup binding one flat branch does not bind:
 the refused forms `/test`, `a//b`, `.`, `..`, `test/../main`, an empty
 argument, `:`, `:rr`, `origin:rr/`, `origin:..`, and a remote half holding a
 `/`, each in the default listing, `--list`, `-r`, `-A`, and `--delete`; and the
 check running where each prefix is taken, so a valid prefix ahead of a refused
 one keeps the rows it printed and the refs it deleted. Needs the nested ref
 name, the alias, and the two remote refs the cited test builds. The two
 whole-remote forms this check accepts are `refs/prefix-whole-remote` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-through-a-ref-file
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO $BRANCH/x
expect-exit: 1
expect-stdout: empty
expect-stderr: contains "error: i/o error: Not a directory (os error 20)"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Listing refs: fstatat(refs/heads/$BRANCH/x): Not a directory"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text refs-bytes
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a PREFIX whose path under `refs/` runs through a ref file is refused before
 it matches anything, so `$BRANCH/x` over the ref `$BRANCH` ends the listing
 rather than selecting the empty set. Both refuse it and exit 1, in different
 words: the tool names the path and the syscall and the port reports the one
 message it gives that condition, so `stderr-text` is not an oracle here
 (`cli-surface.md`, "P1"). `refs-bytes` states that neither side touched a ref.
 The remaining forms and paths are `refs/prefix-through-a-ref-file-forms` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-through-a-ref-file-forms
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_refuses_a_prefix_through_a_ref_file
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the paths a setup binding one flat branch does not bind: a prefix reaching
 a ref file through an alias symlink and one reaching a ref file under
 `refs/remotes/<remote>`, each in the default listing, `--list`, `-r`, `-A`, and
 `--delete`; and the ordering the check keeps under `--delete`, where the refs a
 valid prefix ahead of the refused one removed stand and the prefix behind it
 never runs, so both leave the same refs tree. Needs the nested ref name, the
 alias, and the two remote refs the cited test builds.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/prefix-whole-remote
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_whole_remote_prefix_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a whole-remote PREFIX -- `<remote>:` or `<remote>:.` -- selects every ref
 of the remote it names, and one matching no ref exits 0. The default listing and
 `-r` agree verbatim over the selected refs, compared form by form in
 `refs_listing_matches_the_tool`, which `refs/nested-prefix-stripping` below
 cites for the stripping a non-empty ref half carries. `--list` and `-A` select
 the same refs and name them differently:
 the tool joins the prefix's ref half with the name below it, keeping the `.` of
 that join, so `stdout-text` is not an oracle for those two forms
 (`cli-surface.md`, "P1"). Needs the two remote refs and the remote alias the
 cited test builds.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/alias-listing-empty
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -A
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: empty
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: `-A` lists the aliases alone, so a repository holding none prints
 nothing. The alias round trip -- creating one and listing it -- is
 `refs/alias-round-trip` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/collections-none
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -c
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: empty
oracle: exit-status stdout-text
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the setup creates the repository with no `collection-id` and writes no
 mirror ref, so neither source of a collection ref is present and both
 implementations print nothing. That a local ref is listed once the repository
 carries a `collection-id` is `refs/collections-listing` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/nested-prefix-stripping
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_listing_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the nested-name cases a `run:` line cannot reach against a setup binding
 one flat branch: a PREFIX the ref is nested under printing the name below it,
 `--list` suppressing that, more than one PREFIX grouping the output in
 argument order, a remote ref printed as `remote:name` and keeping its
 `remote:` part when stripped, and the sort order over the whole set. The cited
 test builds the repository the cases need and compares the port's output to
 the tool's for each form.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/alias-round-trip
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_alias_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: `-A --create` writing a relative symlink, `-A` listing it as
 `name -> target` with the leading `../` removed -- or as the target ref's
 refspec, where the link leaves the alias's own ref root -- a PREFIX naming a ref
 exactly printing that ref with its commit checksum in the target position, the
 default listing resolving the alias, and `-A --create` refusing a target that
 names a commit rather than a ref. An interleaved create-then-list sequence,
 which one `run:` line does not state. Also the step the remote-NEWREF refusal
 `refs/create-alias-remote-newref` below states occupies: it is reported over an
 unresolvable positional and over an existence check `--force` suppressed, and a
 NEWREF the ref rule refuses stops one step earlier, which needs the remote ref
 and the second branch the setup does not bind. The cited test also records two
 divergences (`cli-surface.md`, "P1"): the tool names an alias under
 `refs/remotes/<remote>/` by its path below the remote and the port names it by
 its `remote:name` refspec; and `-A --create` naming a target under
 `refs/remotes` makes the tool write a link body naming no file under
 `refs/heads`, where the port writes the path to the target ref's file.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/collections-listing
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::refs_collections_match_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: `-c` listing `(collection-id, ref)` pairs over a repository carrying a
 `collection-id` and a mirror ref, `-c -r` appending the checksum, a positional
 read as a collection id, `-c` winning over `-A`, and `-c --delete` removing a
 whole collection. The setup binds no `collection-id`, so the cited test builds
 the repository.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# refs -- creating and deleting.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO --create=fresh $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: empty
oracle: exit-status stdout-text refs-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: `refs-bytes` states that both implementations left the same set of ref
 files, each holding the checksum its own side committed.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-nested-newref
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO --create=a/b/c $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status refs-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a NEWREF holding `/` has its parent directories created by both.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-remote-newref
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO --create=origin:fresh $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
oracle: exit-status refs-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a `remote:name` NEWREF is written under `refs/remotes/<remote>/`, which
 `refs-bytes` reads as a path rather than as a name.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-alias-remote-newref
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -A --create=origin:al $BRANCH
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: Cannot create alias to remote ref: origin"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Cannot create alias to remote ref: origin"
oracle: exit-status stderr-text refs-bytes
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: an alias lives under `refs/heads`, so the `remote:name` NEWREF the cell
 above writes as a ref is refused under `-A` by both, in the same words, which
 name the remote half alone. The setup binds no remote and the message carries
 the name as given, so the refusal needs none. `refs-bytes` states that neither
 wrote a link. The remaining cases of the step -- the message naming the remote
 half of a nested name, the two steps ahead of it, and the positional it
 precedes -- are in `refs/alias-round-trip` above.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-alias-refused-target
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -A --create=al a/../b
expect-exit: 1
expect-stdout: empty
expect-stderr: contains "error: Cannot create alias to non-existent ref: a/../b"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Cannot create alias to non-existent ref: a/../b"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text stderr-text refs-bytes
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: the target of `-A --create` is answered by an existence check that stands
 ahead of ref-name validation, so a name the ref rule refuses is reported as a
 name no ref holds and no `Invalid refspec` line reaches this site. The name is
 read as given and names no ref in any repository, so the setup binds everything
 the invocation reads. `refs-bytes` states that neither wrote a link. The other
 refused shapes, and the target the tool's own character class refuses, are in
 `refs/invalid-refspec-forms` below.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-alias-target-through-a-ref-file
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -A --create=al $BRANCH/x
expect-exit: 1
expect-stdout: empty
expect-stderr: contains "error: i/o error: Not a directory (os error 20)"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Cannot create alias to non-existent ref: $BRANCH/x"
oracle: exit-status stdout-text refs-bytes
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a target whose path under `refs/` runs through a ref file is refused by
 both and in different words: the tool's existence check reports the target as a
 name no ref holds, where every other site names the path and the syscall, and
 the port reports the one message it gives that condition, so `stderr-text` is
 not an oracle here (`cli-surface.md`, "P1"). A target naming a directory takes
 the same split and needs a ref directory the setup does not bind, so it sits in
 `refs/invalid-refspec-forms` below. `refs-bytes` states that neither wrote a
 link.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-collection
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -c --create=org.example.Fresh:new $BRANCH
expect-exit: 0
expect-stdout: empty
ref-expect-exit: 0
ref-expect-stdout: empty
oracle: exit-status stdout-text refs-bytes
outcome: full
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: with `-c` the NEWREF is a `<collection-id>:<ref>` pair and the write lands
 under `refs/mirrors/<collection-id>/`, which `refs-bytes` reads as a path.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-collection-invalid-id
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -c --create=fresh $BRANCH
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid collection ID fresh"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid collection ID fresh"
oracle: exit-status stderr-text refs-bytes
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
spec: format-reference.md#refs
note: a NEWREF holding no `:` is read as the collection id alone, and `fresh` is
 one element where a collection id takes two or more. `refs-bytes` states that
 neither side wrote a ref of that name.

family: M10
subcommand: refs
cell: refs/create-collection-bad-pair
setup: repo-with-commit
run: refs --repo=$REPO -c --create=org.example.Fresh:a:b $BRANCH
expect-exit: 1
expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid refspec org.example.Fresh:a:b"
ref-expect-exit: 1
ref-expect-stderr: contains "error: Invalid refspec org.example.Fresh:a:b"
oracle: exit-status stderr-text refs-bytes
